==== Solution

Assume the conjunction and obtain a contradiction in each of the two cases supplied by the premise:

|| Line
|| Formula
|| Justification

| 1
| $\neg\phi\lor\neg\psi$
| Premise

| 2
| $\phi\land\psi$
| Assume locally

| 3
| $\phi$
| <Conjunction elimination rule>, left, 2

| 4
| $\psi$
| <Conjunction elimination rule>, right, 2

| 5
| $\neg\phi$
| Assume first case

| 6
| $\bot$
| <Negation elimination rule>, 3 and 5

| 7
| $\neg\psi$
| Assume second case

| 8
| $\bot$
| <Negation elimination rule>, 4 and 7

| 9
| $\bot$
| <Disjunction elimination rule>, 1, 5–6 and 7–8

| 10
| $\neg(\phi\land\psi)$
| <Negation introduction rule>, discharging 2–9

The two case assumptions disappear at line 9, while the conjunction assumption remains available until line 10. The <proof> uses only the primitive rules printed in the paper.

