Both local negated assumptions have been discharged. The <proof> establishes the positive conjunction without assuming an unproved derived version of <De Morgan's laws>.

=== e
{scope}

==== Solution

Write $H$ for $(\phi\to\psi)\to\phi$. The following derives <Peirce's law> directly from the printed primitive rules, including <double-negation elimination>:

|| Line
|| Formula
|| Justification

| 1
| $H$
| Assume for the final implication

| 2
| $\neg\phi$
| Assume locally

| 3
| $\phi$
| Assume inside the scope of 2

| 4
| $\bot$
| <Negation elimination rule>, 2 and 3

| 5
| $\psi$
| <Logical falsity elimination rule>, 4

| 6
| $\phi\to\psi$
| <Implication introduction rule>, discharging 3–5

| 7
| $\phi$
| <Implication elimination rule>, 1 and 6

| 8
| $\bot$
| <Negation elimination rule>, 2 and 7

| 9
| $\neg\neg\phi$
| <Negation introduction rule>, discharging 2–8

| 10
| $\phi$
| <Double-negation elimination>, 9

| 11
| $((\phi\to\psi)\to\phi)\to\phi$
| <Implication introduction rule>, discharging 1–10

Every assumption is discharged, so the last formula is a theorem. In particular, no unproved appeal to the derived `RAA` rule is hidden in the argument.

== 8
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

In a finite domain $\{d_1,\ldots,d_n\}$, a true existential premise says that at least one $d_j$ satisfies $\phi$. The <subproof> treats its witness as an unknown object named $v$, derives $\kappa$ without relying on which object was chosen, and can therefore be applied to the actual witness. Thus $\kappa$ follows whenever the existential premise is true. This argument does not require knowing the domain's size or selecting a particular named element in advance.

The <existential quantifier elimination rule> in sequent form records the same <assumption discharge>: a derivation of $\Gamma,\phi(v)\vdash\kappa$ yields $\Gamma,\exists x\,\phi(x)\vdash\kappa$. The natural-deduction box represents the temporary witness assumption that the sequent rule replaces by the existential premise.

The <eigenvariable condition> requires that $v$ have no free occurrence in the other undischarged assumptions, the existential premise or the exported conclusion $\kappa$. Bound occurrences can be renamed harmlessly. This freshness condition keeps $v$ from denoting a previously special object and prevents <witness escape>. Without it, one could export $S(v)$ from $\exists x\,S(x)$, falsely asserting that an arbitrarily named object has the witness property. A two-element domain with only one element satisfying $S$ supplies an immediate counterexample.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

**The displayed derivation is invalid, although its final conjecture is valid.** Its existential-elimination step exports $T(x_0)$ with the local witness name still free, violating the <eigenvariable condition>. Introducing an existential quantifier afterwards does not repair that earlier invalid rule application.

Move the <existential quantifier introduction rule> inside the witness scope:

|| Line
|| Formula
|| Justification

| 1
| $\forall x\,(S(x)\to T(x))$
| Premise

| 2
| $\exists x\,S(x)$
| Premise

| 3
| $S(u)$
| Assume with fresh $u$

| 4
| $S(u)\to T(u)$
| <Universal quantifier elimination rule>, 1

| 5
| $T(u)$
| <Implication elimination rule>, 3 and 4

| 6
| $\exists x\,T(x)$
| <Existential quantifier introduction rule>, 5

| 7
| $\exists x\,T(x)$
| <Existential quantifier elimination rule>, 2 and 3–6

The exported formula contains no free $u$, and neither original premise contains that name. The witness assumption is now discharged legally.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

For readability write $R(s,t)$ for the uninterpreted relation $s\ni t$. In the first <proof>, introduce the universal quantifier *inside* the existential witness scope:

|| Line
|| Formula
|| Justification

| 1
| $\exists y\,\forall x\,R(x,y)$
| Premise

| 2
| $\forall x\,R(x,w)$
| Assume with fresh witness name $w$

| 3
| $R(u,w)$
| <Universal quantifier elimination rule>, 2; choose fresh arbitrary $u$

| 4
| $\exists y\,R(u,y)$
| <Existential quantifier introduction rule>, 3

| 5
| $\forall x\,\exists y\,R(x,y)$
| <Universal quantifier introduction rule>, 4; $u$ is absent from open assumptions

| 6
| $\forall x\,\exists y\,R(x,y)$
| <Existential quantifier elimination rule>, 1 and 2–5

In the second <proof>, first close the witness scope and *then* generalize the arbitrary object:

|| Line
|| Formula
|| Justification

| 1
| $\exists y\,\forall x\,R(x,y)$
| Premise; choose $u$ arbitrary and fresh for this premise

| 2
| $\forall x\,R(x,w)$
| Assume with fresh $w$, also different from $u$

| 3
| $R(u,w)$
| <Universal quantifier elimination rule>, 2

| 4
| $\exists y\,R(u,y)$
| <Existential quantifier introduction rule>, 3

| 5
| $\exists y\,R(u,y)$
| <Existential quantifier elimination rule>, 1 and 2–4

| 6
| $\forall x\,\exists y\,R(x,y)$
| <Universal quantifier introduction rule>, 5

These are distinct <proof> trees: the final two rule applications are interchanged, and their freshness conditions have been checked in their respective contexts. They express the <quantifier order and uniform witness> principle: a single witness working for every object also supplies a witness for each object individually.

**The converse is not valid.** In a domain $\{0,1\}$ interpret $R(s,t)$ as equality of its arguments. Every object has a witness, namely itself, but no one object equals both 0 and 1. The premise of the converse is true and its conclusion false. The symbol $\ni$ is an arbitrary binary relation here; no set-membership axioms may be imposed on it without an additional premise.

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

Let $K$ abbreviate $Q(c_1)\lor Q(c_2)$, and group the three equality alternatives on the left. The <proof> uses the binary <disjunction elimination rule> twice, so no unproved three-way derived rule is needed.

|| Line
|| Formula
|| Justification

| 1
| $\forall x\,((x=c_0\lor x=c_1)\lor x=c_2)$
| Premise

| 2
| $\neg Q(c_0)$
| Assume for the first implication

| 3
| $\exists x\,Q(x)$
| Assume for the second implication

| 4
| $Q(u)$
| Assume with fresh witness name $u$

| 5
| $(u=c_0\lor u=c_1)\lor u=c_2$
| <Universal quantifier elimination rule>, 1

| 6
| $u=c_0\lor u=c_1$
| Assume the first outer case

| 7
| $u=c_0$
| Assume the first inner case

| 8
| $Q(c_0)$
| <Equality elimination rule>, 7 and 4

| 9
| $\bot$
| <Negation elimination rule>, 2 and 8

| 10
| $K$
| <Logical falsity elimination rule>, 9

| 11
| $u=c_1$
| Assume the second inner case

| 12
| $Q(c_1)$
| <Equality elimination rule>, 11 and 4

| 13
| $K$
| <Disjunction introduction rule>, left, 12

| 14
| $K$
| <Disjunction elimination rule>, 6, 7–10 and 11–13

| 15
| $u=c_2$
| Assume the second outer case

| 16
| $Q(c_2)$
| <Equality elimination rule>, 15 and 4

| 17
| $K$
| <Disjunction introduction rule>, right, 16

| 18
| $K$
| <Disjunction elimination rule>, 5, 6–14 and 15–17

| 19
| $K$
| <Existential quantifier elimination rule>, 3 and 4–18

| 20
| $(\exists x\,Q(x))\to K$
| <Implication introduction rule>, discharging 3–19

| 21
| $\neg Q(c_0)\to(\exists x\,Q(x))\to K$
| <Implication introduction rule>, discharging 2–20

The witness name is absent from $K$ and from every assumption remaining after line 19, so the existential elimination is legal. Each equality is used in the forward direction supplied by the printed equality rule. The three named constants need not denote distinct objects: the case <proof> requires only that they exhaust the domain. This is <existential quantification over a finitely named domain> followed by exclusion of the first case.
